   "line": [
    "c4",
    "e6"
   ],
   "spoken": "Another Black game — the Queen's Gambit Declined structure, e6. A big benefit of being a QGD player: you barely need to study the English."
  },
  {
   "ply": 3,
   "t": 638.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pppp1ppp/4p3/8/2P5/5N2/PP1PPPPP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 1 2",
   "line": [
    "Nf3"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 4,
   "t": 639.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/ppp2ppp/4p3/3p4/2P5/5N2/PP1PPPPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 3",
   "line": [
    "d5"
   ],
   "spoken": "White has few ways to avoid the QGD — most English players end up with d4. Black plays d5."
  },
  {
   "ply": 5,
   "t": 646,
   "fen": "rnbqkbnr/ppp2ppp/4p3/3p4/2P5/5NP1/PP1PPP1P/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 0 3",
   "line": [
    "g3"
   ],
   "spoken": "White handles it in English style with g3 — likely transposing to a Catalan if d4 comes, or a reversed Benoni with b3."
  },
  {
   "ply": 6,
   "t": 659.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/4pn2/3p4/2P5/5NP1/PP1PPP1P/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 1 4",
   "line": [
    "Nf6"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 664,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/4pn2/3P4/8/5NP1/PP1PPP1P/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 0 4",
   "line": [
    "cxd5"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 8,
   "t": 668,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/5n2/3p4/8/5NP1/PP1PPP1P/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "exd5"
   ],
   "spoken": "But taking on d5 prematurely is a bad move — it releases the central tension, hands Black better control of the centre, and is completely toothless."
  },
  {
   "ply": 9,
   "t": 678.5,
   "fen": "rnbqkb1r/ppp2ppp/5n2/3p4/3P4/5NP1/PP2PP1P/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 0 5",
   "line": [
    "d4"
   ],
   "spoken": "Now, how to place the minor pieces — there is no single right answer."
  },
  {
   "ply": 11,
   "t": 703.5,
   "fen": "rnbqk2r/ppp2ppp/3b1n2/3p4/3P4/5NP1/PP2PPBP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 2 6",
   "line": [
    "Bd6",
    "Bg2"
   ],
   "spoken": "I am partial to the bishop to d6, London-style, followed later by c6."
  },
  {
   "ply": 12,
   "t": 708.5,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/ppp2ppp/3b1n2/3p4/3P4/5NP1/PP2PPBP/RNBQK2R w KQ - 3 7",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": "Castle first — this is not a sharp Najdorf, so the move order is flexible."
  },
  {
   "ply": 13,
   "t": 717,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/ppp2ppp/3b1n2/3p4/3P4/5NP1/PP2PPBP/RNBQ1RK1 b - - 4 7",
   "line": [
    "O-O"
   ],
   "spoken": ""
  },
  {
   "ply": 14,
   "t": 719,
   "fen": "rnbq1rk1/pp3ppp/2pb1n2/3p4/3P4/5NP1/PP2PPBP/RNBQ1RK1 w - - 0 8",
   "line": [
    "c6"
   ],
   "spoken": "Then c6 — a lovely structure with the bishop in the middle. Where does the light bishop go? If you are a London player, you lean toward f5. White prevents it with the queen to c2 — no problem. The flexible move is the rook to e8: it is good there and it stops the knight to e5."
  },
  {
   "ply": 17,
   "t": 803.5,
   "fen": "rnbqr1k1/pp3ppp/2pb1n2/3p4/3P4/2N2NP1/PPQ1PPBP/R1B2RK1 b - - 3 9",
   "line": [
    "Nc3",
    "Re8",
    "Qc2"
   ],
   "spoken": "There is a typical maneuver to reach that diagonal without the weakening g6: the bishop to g4, then swing it via h5 toward g6. A subtle point too — you can allow White's b4 and answer a6, freezing the pawn, rather than automatically playing a5. Then h6 forces the bishop to decide, and if the bishop goes to e3 it is a blunder: the pawn on f2 that defends it is overloaded, since it also holds g3. And the hallmark of great attackers like Tal and Morphy is knowing when to just play simple and win — the clean order here is the knight takes on f2 first, then after the recapture the bishop takes on f3, and White cannot recapture in any of three ways."
  },
  {
